@{
    ViewBag.Title = "JavaScript Rules";
}
<div class="col-sm-12">
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">All code must pass the rules of jsHint</h2>
        <p>jsHint will ensure that your JavaScript code follows some coding conventions and standards to meet the expected quality.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Using namespacing and object creation patterns in your code</h2>
        <p>
            There are some reasons that you should apply namespacing pattern in your code:
            <ul>
                <li>Help to avoid collisions with other objects or variables in the global namespace</li>
                <li>You can declare your object in local scope.</li>
                <li>Result in nearly the same experience with one when coding with C#</li>
            </ul>
            <br />
            In the following code, we use a library named <code>namespace</code> to register our class to a specific namespace.
        </p>
        <div class="example-code">
            <pre class="text-success">
                /* Recommended */
                Namespace.Register('ONEbook.FrontEndGuidelines.JavaScriptRules');
                ONEbook.FrontEndGuidelines.JavaScriptRules = function () {
                    var me = this;

                    // Properties and functions come here.
                };
            </pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Remove all trailing whitespaces</h2>
        <p>
            The number of whitespaces for a tab may vary with many editors, thus using whitespace instead of tab helps to keep the format of code be the same.
            Do not use tabs or mix tabs and spaces for indentation.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Use spaces instead of tabs</h2>
        <p>The trailing whitespaces because they are unnecessary and can complicate diffs.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Use single quotation marks for string values</h2>
        <div class="example-code">
            <pre class="text-danger">
                /* Not recommended */
                var exampleText = "example text";
            </pre>
            <pre class="text-success">
                /* Recommended */
                var exampleText = 'example text';
            </pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Avoid DOM manipulation as possible</h2>
        <p>
            Make sure that you are manipulating elements fragments just before they are added to the DOM.
            It will help to speed up JavaScript performance.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Avoid using global variables</h2>
        <p>
            Variables in the local scope will be found more quickly. They are destroyed when the local scope is lost.
            The memory they use can be freed up by the garbage collector.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Use function inlining</h2>
        <p>
            Function inlining is a powerful high level optimization which eliminates call cost
            and increase the chances of other optimization taking effect due to the breaking down of the call boundaries.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Modify an invisible element</h2>
        <p>
            When an element is invisible, it will not need to repaint to the screen, even if its contents are changed, since it is not being displayed.
            This can be used as an advantage if we make several changes to an invisible element or its contents before repainting it.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Making several style changes at once</h2>
        <p>
            Using CSS classes rather than changing each property one by one to limit the times the browser refreshes your presentation.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-section">
        <h2 class="sub-title">Cache DOM elements that are commonly used</h2>
        <p>
            Caching DOM elements will help to improve the performance by avoiding querying DOM many times.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
@section scripts
{
    @Scripts.Render("~/bundles/rules/js")
}